# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Critical Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET 5+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` also throws** — both are completely removed
3. **`ThreadAbortException` will never be raised** by the runtime

The entire cancellation model must change to **`CancellationToken`** cooperative cancellation.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();

    public void Start()
    {
        _workerThread = new Thread(ProcessLoop) { IsBackground = true };
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop()
    {
        var token = _cts.Token;
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls can be interrupted
                var item = GetNextItem(token);
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — roll back partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit the loop cleanly; finally still runs
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait for the thread to finish (replaces Sleep + Abort)
        if (_workerThread?.IsAlive == true)
        {
            bool exited = _workerThread.Join(timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
            if (!exited)
            {
                // Log a warning — there is no safe forced-kill equivalent.
                // If this happens, your blocking call isn't observing the token.
            }
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Migration Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Blocking calls that ignore the token** | If `GetNextItem()` blocks on I/O or a `Monitor.Wait`, cancellation won't interrupt it. You *must* thread the `CancellationToken` into every blocking API. |
| **No forced kill exists** | `Thread.Abort()` had no safe replacement *on purpose*. If you truly need a kill-switch, isolate work in a **separate process** and terminate the process. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort()` compiles but throws** | Code referencing it will build against .NET 8 but crash at runtime — it's a runtime-only `PlatformNotSupportedException`. |
| **`Thread.Join()` vs `Thread.Sleep()`** | The original `Sleep(2000)` was a race condition. `Join(timeout)` deterministically waits for the thread to exit. |
| **`volatile bool` is no longer enough** | `CancellationToken` replaces the `volatile bool` pattern and also enables waking up blocked calls via `token.Register()` or token-aware APIs. |

## Alternate: Use `BackgroundService` (preferred for hosted apps)

If this runs inside ASP.NET Core or a Generic Host, use the built-in pattern:

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : BackgroundService
{
    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        while (!stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(stoppingToken);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, stoppingToken);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This gives you dependency injection, graceful shutdown via `IHostApplicationLifetime`, and health checks for free — no manual thread management needed.
